{{ macros.section(3, "Reference", id="reference") }}

<div class="def-cards">
  <details class="def-card" id="pragma-recipe_join">
    <summary class="def-card-head"><span class="def-card-name"><code>recipe_join</code></span><span class="def-card-kind">compile</span></summary>
    <div class="def-card-body">
      <p>Selects the <a href="{{_}}/cmk/compiler#recipe-body-joining">recipe-body joining</a> connector: how the newline-separated lines of one recipe body are combined into a single shell.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>&amp;&amp;</code>: <em>(default)</em> fail-fast; the body shares one shell and stops on the first error.</li>
        <li><code>;</code>: run-all; the body still shares one shell but does not stop on failure.</li>
        <li><code>none</code>: no join: each line becomes its own recipe-line (classic <code>make</code>), with <strong>no shared shell state</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Caution with <code>none</code>:</strong> with no shared shell across lines, anything that relies on it stops working: <code>cd</code> between lines, the <a href="{{_}}/cmk/compiler#capture-operator"><code>&lt;-</code> operator</a>, and the <code>&amp;&amp;</code>-chained decorator forms.  Use it only when you specifically want classic per-line <code>make</code> semantics.</p>
      <p class="def-card-see"><a href="{{_}}/cmk/compiler#recipe-body-joining">Recipe-Body Joining</a></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="def-card" id="pragma-plugin_pragma_allowed">
    <summary class="def-card-head"><span class="def-card-name"><code>plugin_pragma_allowed</code></span><span class="def-card-kind">compile</span></summary>
    <div class="def-card-body">
      <p>By default an imported plugin has <strong>zero</strong> influence on how its importer compiles, since pragma is strictly a property of the entry program.  This gate opts the entry into a <strong>two-pass</strong> compile where its imported plugins' pragma can alter the importer's own compilation.</p>
      <p>With the gate set, <code>mk.compile</code> runs a discover+merge phase (the <code>__pragma__.resolve</code> target) before the real compile:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Discover:</strong> compile the entry once in register-only discovery mode (populates the import list but skips code-gen), then re-parse so the <em>real</em> imports populate <code>${__plugins__.paths}</code> (computed / conditional / <code>foreach</code> imports resolve for real, no shadow parser).</li>
        <li><strong>Merge:</strong> read each discovered plugin's pragma and merge it, <strong>strictly additive</strong>: a key that appears in more than one candidate is a <strong>hard error</strong>, so stacking can only <em>add</em> settings, never quietly override one.</li>
        <li><strong>Recompile</strong> with the merged manifest.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The proven example: a plugin whose <code>recipe_join: ";"</code> flips the <em>importer's</em> joiner from the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> default to run-all.  Without the gate that plugin pragma is inert.</p>
      <p class="def-card-see"><a href="{{_}}/plugins/overview#program-self-models">Program Self-Models</a></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="def-card" id="pragma-compiler_pre_post">
    <summary class="def-card-head"><span class="def-card-name"><code>compiler_pre</code> / <code>compiler_post</code></span><span class="def-card-kind">compile</span></summary>
    <div class="def-card-body">
      <p>The preprocessor runs a fixed chain of stages (<code>minify decorators dialect cmkanchor dedent moduledoc sugar &hellip; unsentinel</code>).  These two <strong>list</strong> settings splice extra stages around it: <code>compiler_pre</code> prepends, <code>compiler_post</code> appends.</p>
      <pre><code># cmk_pragma ::: { "compiler_pre": ["mystage"], "compiler_post": ["cleanup"] } :::</code></pre>
      <p>Both <strong>accumulate</strong> (read via <code>__pragma__.append</code>): the pragma <strong>and</strong> the matching env var (<code>CMK_COMPILER_PRE</code> / <code>CMK_COMPILER_POST</code>) both contribute, like <code>+=</code>.  With neither set the effective chain is byte-identical to the core one, so it costs nothing by default.</p>
      <p>An injected name resolves one of two ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>a <strong>core</strong> <code>.cmk.&lt;name&gt;</code> stage macro (plus an <code>lang.comp.pipeline.&lt;name&gt;</code> target on the stepwise debug path), or</li>
        <li>a <strong>plugin-provided</strong> stage: a <code>_cmk_blk_&lt;name&gt;</code> awk <code>define</code> shipped by a <code>.cmk</code> on <code>CMK_PLUGINS_DIR</code>.  Since plugins are not loaded during compilation, the block is <strong>lifted</strong> out of the file (extracted to a temp and run as <code>awk -f</code>).  A name resolving to neither is skipped with a warning.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong><code>vm_hydrate: true</code></strong> is a back-compat alias for <code>compiler_pre += vm_hydrate</code>, whose stage body is <code>_cmk_blk_vm_hydrate</code> lifted from <code>virtual-machine.cmk</code>, so the core compiler no longer hard-codes any VM concept.</p>
      <p class="def-card-see"><a href="{{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming#about-sugar">Sugar &amp; Stages</a></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="def-card" id="pragma-target_locals">
    <summary class="def-card-head"><span class="def-card-name"><code>target_locals</code></span><span class="def-card-kind">compile</span></summary>
    <div class="def-card-body">
      <p>When truthy, recipes that reference <code>__locals__</code> (or a <code>*.locals</code> convenience wrapper) get a <strong>baseline-capture preamble</strong> injected as their first recipe line: a snapshot of the shell environment at recipe entry.  It costs nothing for recipes that never reference it.</p>
      <p><code>${cmk.__locals__()}</code> then yields a JSON object of the vars <em>this</em> recipe defined: current shell vars minus that baseline (so it captures <code>&lt;-</code> vars and <code>export</code>s alike).  It expands to a shell snippet, so use it via <code>&lt;-</code> or backticks, not as a bare arg.  Without the pragma, <code>__locals__</code> prints <code>{}</code> and a warning.</p>
      <p class="def-card-see"><a href="{{_}}/cmk/quickref#reflection">Reflection</a></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="def-card" id="pragma-lifecycle">
    <summary class="def-card-head"><span class="def-card-name">Boot lifecycle</span><span class="def-card-kind">boot</span></summary>
    <div class="def-card-body">
      <p>Five pragmas configure a program's boot <strong>lifecycle</strong> (before -&gt; main -&gt; after) rather than its compilation:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>bootloaders</code>: inline bash <code>define</code>s <code>source</code>d in the supervisor's shell before make (single <code>$</code>; can register an <code>EXIT</code> trap).</li>
        <li><code>cmk_pre</code> / <code>cmk_post</code>: make targets run before / after the main pipeline (a failing <code>cmk_pre</code> aborts before main; <code>cmk_post</code> still runs).  Three accumulating surfaces each: pragma, env (<code>CMK_PRE</code>/<code>CMK_POST</code>), and the <code>__cmk_pre__</code>/<code>__cmk_post__</code> parse-time handle.</li>
        <li><code>hooks: off</code>: disables the <code>&lt;target&gt;.pre</code>/<code>.post</code> target-rewriting.</li>
        <li><code>bootloader_disabled</code>: skips the bootloader entirely.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These take effect only via <code>cmk run</code> (the path that owns the re-exec) and thread into the canonical <code>CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS</code>/<code>CMK_BOOTLOADER*</code> env, not the <code>CMK_PRAGMA_*</code> namespace, because the bash header reads them before make exists.  The one boot setting a pragma can <em>never</em> set is the supervisor (<code>CMK_SUPERVISOR</code>).</p>
      <p class="def-card-see"><a href="{{_}}/runtime/#lifecycle-boot-settings">Lifecycle &amp; Boot Settings</a> (Runtime)</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</div>

