{{ macros.section(3, "Example: REPL Mode", id="repl") }}
<a id="pragma-repl"></a>

Where the compile pragmas shape how a program *compiles* and the boot settings how it *boots*, `repl` declares how it *runs*: the program's **default action** becomes an interactive REPL over its own target namespace, rather than a single pass of `__main__`.  Turn `repl` on and `cmk run` launches the harness; typing a target name runs it (think of `python`'s interactive mode, or `irb`, for a `.cmk`).  It needs no import or launcher, since the REPL harness is built into the core dispatcher.

The object form wires the harness's three regions (a scrolling **print** stream, the **eval** input sink, and a one-line **read** mode-line) to the program's own targets:

```yaml
repl: { read: <t>, eval: <t>, print: <t>, exit_after: 1 }
```

The reflective overlay in {{ macros.repo_link('demos/cmk/overlay.cmk', name='overlay.cmk', github=github) }} wires exactly this.  Naming a target explicitly **bypasses** the mode; the `repl` subcommand **forces** it on any program.  Either way only the runtime applies it, since it owns the harness.  See also the [Virtual Machine]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine).

The REPL harness in action, a program whose only pragma flips it into an interactive shell over its own targets:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/repl.cmk', github=github)}}

This completes the **dialect + sugar + pragma** triad.  A natural next step (not yet implemented) is bundling all three under one named language, selectable in a single line (the way Racket's `#lang` names a whole language) so a project-specific DSL can be reused across files without repeating its hints.

